 mara:[FIC,['The kitchen smelled of cinnamon.','Mara checked the clock for the third time in ten minutes and smoothed the tablecloth twice.','Outside, a dog barked at the bicycles going by.']],
 theo:[FIC,['Theo’s boots were caked with mud, and his sleeves were wet to the elbow.','He set the lantern down gently, as if it might break, and held his palms to the stove.','“The river’s higher than it was,” he said, not looking up.']],
 lena:[FIC,['At the market, Lena counted her coins twice and put the blue ribbon back on the table.','“It’s too plain anyway,” she told the seller, and she walked off before he could answer.']],
 frostEnd:['Robert Frost, “The Road Not Taken” (1916), last stanza',['I shall be telling this with a sigh','Somewhere ages and ages hence:','Two roads diverged in a wood, and I—','I took the one less traveled by,','And that has made all the difference.'],1],
 lantern:[FIC,['A village kept one lantern burning on the cliff so that fishing boats could find the harbor.','One stormy night the keeper’s oil ran low, and he began to burn his own chairs, then his table, then his door, rather than let the light go out.','By dawn every boat was home, and the keeper sat in a cold, empty house, smiling.']],
 sparrows:[FIC,['Two sparrows found a single crust of bread.','The first snatched it and flew off, and the second chased her, pecking and scolding.','While they quarreled, the crust fell to the ground, and a crow carried it away.','“We could have shared it,” said the first sparrow. “We could have,” said the second.']],
 ines:[FIC,['Ines hid her poor marks from her family.','She tore the report from her bag, then told her sister a lie to cover the first.','By winter the lies had grown so many that she stopped speaking at dinner.','When she finally showed her father the marks, he only said, “I wish you’d told me sooner,” and Ines felt lighter than she had in months.']],
 wolf:['Aesop, “The Boy Who Cried Wolf” (an ancient Greek fable, retold here)',['A bored shepherd boy cried “Wolf!” to see the villagers run up the hill, and he laughed when they came.','He did it again the next day.','When a real wolf came, he cried out in earnest, but no one came, and the wolf scattered his flock.']],
 tortoise:['Aesop, “The Tortoise and the Hare” (an ancient Greek fable, retold here)',['The hare laughed at the slow tortoise and challenged him to a race.','Sure of winning, the hare lay down for a nap halfway through.','The tortoise kept walking at the same steady pace and crossed the finish line first.']],
 dev:[FIC,['Dev always arrived early to set out the chairs.','When the new student sat alone at lunch, Dev slid his own tray across the table and asked, “Do you like chess?”']],
